{:edb/db-before-t 1574 :edb/db-after-t 1575 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534887 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:09:12.159Z" 13194139534887 true] [17592186078250 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}$." 13194139534887 false] [17592186078250 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186078250 1145 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ\\cot θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot \\cot θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{4\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$,\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Rewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ}|= (1)/((lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin θ}{θ})); = \\frac{1}{1}; = 1$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14$.\nTherefore, we have\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{θ}{\\sin θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{4\\cos θ}|= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}$." 13194139534887 false] [17592186078250 1145 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186078299 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186078299 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x}$." 13194139534887 false] [17592186078299 1145 "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{2\\sin x}{3x} &= lim_(x → 0)(\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534887 false] [17592186078299 1145 "Notice that as $x \\to 0,$ both the numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186078407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317499 13194139534887 true] [17592186078407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317513 13194139534887 true] [17592186078407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317517 13194139534887 true] [17592186078407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317520 13194139534887 true] [17592186078407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317544 13194139534887 true] [17592186078490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317506 13194139534887 true] [17592186078490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317509 13194139534887 true] [17592186078490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317523 13194139534887 true] [17592186078490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317530 13194139534887 true] [17592186078490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317537 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317500 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1089 "q-105433" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1100 "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1113 #uuid "e0552357-0b1d-5f8f-a303-1bf9280ab97c" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317499 1145 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )=4{cos}^{2}⁡0=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =1\\cdot 4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317501 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317502 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317503 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317504 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317505 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1041 "selection" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317503 13194139534887 true] [17592186317500 1110 #uuid "a9bcb693-b589-5999-9b77-e0e7d238488f" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317501 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534887 true] [17592186317501 1111 #uuid "b87a6ab7-70dd-56fb-875c-f3df68c06b5f" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317502 1034 "1" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534887 true] [17592186317502 1111 #uuid "0c075e91-d394-5778-9998-63a0666727cd" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317503 1034 "4" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534887 true] [17592186317503 1111 #uuid "34260e19-3634-5f6f-aa69-2cbbfb984753" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317504 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534887 true] [17592186317504 1111 #uuid "c25786cc-e74a-50fb-a370-24f92618d318" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317505 1034 "0" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534887 true] [17592186317505 1111 #uuid "bb084e61-5b05-579c-b38b-cb7e2fa6562e" 13194139534887 true] [17592186317506 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317507 131